-- ARTICLE 12: How to Identify Genuine Job Opportunities and Avoid Scams
-- ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
(
  'How to Identify Genuine Job Opportunities and Avoid Job Scams in India',
  'how-to-avoid-job-scams-india',
  'Job scams targeting freshers in India are widespread. Learn the warning signs of fake jobs, fraudulent recruiters, and "too good to be true" offers — and how to protect yourself.',
  E'# How to Identify Genuine Job Opportunities and Avoid Job Scams in India\n\nEvery year, thousands of Indian job seekers — many of them recent graduates — lose money and time to fraudulent job listings. Job scams in India range from fake recruitment agencies that collect registration fees, to elaborate offers from companies that do not exist.\n\nKnowing how to verify a job opportunity before you apply — or before you pay anything — is a critical career skill.\n\n## The Most Common Job Scams in India\n\n### 1. Fake Recruitment Agencies\nFraudulent agencies advertise jobs at large, well-known companies. They charge a "registration fee," "processing fee," or "background check fee" to get you the job. Legitimate recruitment agencies are paid by the employer, not the candidate. If anyone asks you to pay to get a job, walk away.\n\n### 2. Fake Appointment Letters\nScammers send professional-looking appointment letters from companies like "Infosys," "TCS," or "Wipro" — with official-looking branding and logos — and ask the candidate to pay for training, equipment, or a security deposit before joining. Real companies never ask new employees to pay anything before joining.\n\n### 3. Work-From-Home Fraud\nListings promising "₹30,000–₹50,000 per month, work from home, part time, no experience needed, flexible hours" are overwhelmingly either scams or data-entry pyramid schemes. Legitimate work-from-home jobs exist, but they come with job descriptions, interviews, and reasonable salary expectations.\n\n### 4. Fake HR on WhatsApp\nFraudulent "HR representatives" contact candidates directly on WhatsApp or Telegram, claiming to be from a major company. They offer a job without an interview and ask for personal documents or money for "onboarding" or "training kit procurement."\n\n### 5. Phishing Job Portals\nSome scammers create websites that look like legitimate job portals and collect personal and banking information under the guise of a job application.\n\n## Red Flags: How to Spot a Fake Job\n\n| Signal | Legitimate | Suspicious |\n|--------|------------|------------|\n| Application process | Structured, with interview rounds | Instant offer with no interview |\n| Recruiter email | Corporate domain (e.g., @infosys.com) | Gmail, Yahoo, or unknown domain |\n| Payment required | Never | "Registration fee," "training fee," "kit fee" |\n| Job description | Specific responsibilities and requirements | Vague or too broad |\n| Salary | Reasonable for role and experience | Unrealistically high |\n| Contact method | Official email or platform | WhatsApp, Telegram only |\n| Offer letter | On official letterhead, after interview | Via WhatsApp/email immediately |\n\n## How to Verify a Job Opportunity\n\n**Step 1: Search the company independently.**\nDo not click links in the job posting. Search the company name on Google separately. Check if the website matches what was advertised. Verify the company on the Ministry of Corporate Affairs website (mca.gov.in) if you want to confirm its legal registration.\n\n**Step 2: Verify the recruiter''s email domain.**\nA recruiter claiming to be from Wipro should have an `@wipro.com` email address. If the email is `wipro.recruitment@gmail.com`, that is a strong warning sign.\n\n**Step 3: Apply through official channels.**\nIf you are interested in a company, apply through their official career page — not through a third-party posting. For example, TCS careers: tcs.com/careers. This eliminates the risk of a fake middleman.\n\n**Step 4: Look the company up on LinkedIn.**\nA real company will have a LinkedIn presence with employees. You can often verify whether the person who contacted you actually works there.\n\n**Step 5: Never pay to get a job.**\nThis cannot be stated clearly enough: no legitimate employer in India charges job seekers to apply, interview, or join. Any payment request is a scam.\n\n**Step 6: Be cautious with personal documents.**\nDo not share your Aadhaar, PAN card, bank account details, or passport before you have a verified offer from a company and you are in the formal onboarding process.\n\n## What to Do if You Encounter a Job Scam\n\n- Do not pay any money\n- Do not share sensitive personal documents\n- Report the listing on the platform where you found it\n- File a complaint at the **National Cyber Crime Reporting Portal**: cybercrime.gov.in\n- Report to the **National Consumer Helpline**: 1800-11-4000\n- Warn your college network and classmates\n\n## How to Search for Jobs Safely\n\n- Use established job portals: Naukri, LinkedIn, Internshala, or curated boards like Career Updates\n- Apply directly through company career pages for roles at known organisations\n- Be sceptical of any opportunity that arrives unsolicited via WhatsApp, SMS, or unknown emails\n- Trust your instincts — if something feels wrong, it probably is\n\n## Final Reminder\n\nA real job offer is the result of a process: you apply, you interview, the company evaluates you, and they make an offer. The company pays you — not the other way around. If anyone tries to reverse that logic, it is a scam.',
  'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1563986768494-4dee2763ff3f?w=800&q=80',
  'Job Search',
  ARRAY['Job Scams', 'Freshers', 'India', 'Safety', 'Job Search'],
  'Career Updates Team',
  'published',
  false,
  'How to Avoid Job Scams in India | Career Updates',
  'Learn how to identify fake job listings, fraudulent recruiters, and job scams in India. Protect yourself with these practical verification steps.',
  now() - interval '6 days'
),

