-- ARTICLE 17: How to Handle Salary Expectations as a Fresher
-- ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
(
  'How to Handle Salary Expectations as a Fresher',
  'how-to-handle-salary-expectations-fresher',
  'Answering the salary question is one of the most stressful moments in a fresher interview. Here is how to research market rates, answer the question confidently, and evaluate an offer.',
  E'# How to Handle Salary Expectations as a Fresher\n\nThe salary question makes most freshers nervous. Ask for too much and you might seem unrealistic. Ask for too little and you leave money on the table — or worse, signal that you do not value your own skills.\n\nHandling the salary conversation well is a practical skill that you can prepare for. This guide explains how.\n\n## Why Companies Ask About Salary Expectations\n\nCompanies ask about salary expectations primarily to:\n1. Confirm you are within their budget before investing more time\n2. Understand how you value yourself and whether your expectation is calibrated\n3. Begin a potential negotiation\n\nFor freshers, many companies — especially large IT service companies — have fixed or near-fixed entry-level packages. In these cases, the salary question is often more of a verification than a negotiation. But at startups and product companies, there is often more room to negotiate.\n\n## Step 1: Research Before the Interview\n\nNever go into a salary conversation without researching the market. Your benchmark should come from reliable sources, not assumptions.\n\n**Where to research:**\n- **Glassdoor.com** — Search for the specific company and role. Look at fresher or entry-level salary reviews.\n- **LinkedIn Salary** — Available in India for many roles.\n- **AmbitionBox** — An India-focused salary database with reviews from Indian employees. Particularly useful for fresher salary benchmarks at specific companies.\n- **Naukri Salary Insights** — Naukri.com provides salary range data for specific job titles and locations.\n- **Talk to seniors** — Your college seniors who are already working at similar companies are one of your best sources of real salary information.\n\n**General fresher salary ranges in India (approximate, subject to change):**\n\n| Company Type | Typical Fresher CTC |\n|---|---|\n| IT service companies (TCS, Infosys, Wipro) | ₹3–4.5 LPA |\n| Mid-size IT companies | ₹4–7 LPA |\n| Product companies (Zoho, Freshworks, Razorpay) | ₹6–15 LPA |\n| Top product companies / startups | ₹15–25 LPA+ |\n| PSUs (based on GATE score) | ₹6–12 LPA (incl. allowances) |\n\nThese are general ranges and vary by year, market conditions, and individual company policies. Always verify with current sources.\n\n## Step 2: Know the Difference Between CTC and Take-Home\n\nWhen a company quotes a package, they usually quote **CTC — Cost to Company**. This is not the amount you receive in your bank account.\n\n**CTC includes:**\n- Basic salary\n- HRA (House Rent Allowance)\n- Special allowance\n- Provident Fund (employer contribution)\n- Variable pay / performance bonus\n- Gratuity\n- Other benefits (medical insurance, food allowance, etc.)\n\n**Take-home salary** (net in-hand) is typically 65–80% of the CTC after deductions (PF, professional tax, TDS).\n\nA ₹4 LPA CTC for a fresher typically means ₹25,000–₹28,000 per month in hand. Always ask for the breakup.\n\n## Step 3: How to Answer the Question in the Interview\n\n### If the company has a fixed package:\nMany large IT companies — TCS iON, Infosys, Wipro — have fixed fresher packages. In these cases, simply acknowledge the standard and confirm you are comfortable:\n\n> "I understand you have a standard fresher package. I am comfortable with the company''s offer and I am more focused on the learning opportunity at this stage."\n\n### If there is flexibility:\nGive a range, not a single number. Make the bottom of your range your actual minimum:\n\n> "Based on my research of the market and for this role in [city], I am targeting somewhere in the range of ₹X–₹Y LPA. I am open to discussing this based on the full compensation package and growth opportunity."\n\n### If asked to name a number first:\nCounteroffer by asking for their budget:\n\n> "I want to make sure we''re aligned. Could you share the budgeted range for this role? I''d like to confirm we''re in the right ballpark."\n\n## Step 4: Evaluating a Job Offer\n\nWhen an offer comes, do not just look at the headline number. Consider:\n\n- **Fixed vs variable component:** A large variable component means you only earn it if you hit targets. Understand this split.\n- **Cost of living:** ₹4 LPA in Tier-2 city goes further than ₹4 LPA in Mumbai or Bangalore.\n- **Growth trajectory:** A lower starting salary at a company with fast internal growth may outperform a higher starting salary at a company with flat increments.\n- **Learning opportunity:** Especially in the first job, what you learn matters more than the specific number.\n- **Benefits:** Health insurance, PF contributions, and leaves are part of your real compensation.\n\n## Can Freshers Negotiate?\n\nYes — but with appropriate expectations.\n\n- At **fixed-package companies** (most large IT service firms), there is typically very limited room to negotiate the base package.\n- At **product companies and startups**, there is often more flexibility, especially if you have demonstrated strong skills.\n- **Negotiation levers** for freshers: joining bonus, role clarity, team preference, start date.\n\nIf you negotiate, be respectful and realistic. Base your ask on market data, not personal preference.\n\n## Final Thoughts\n\nYour first salary is important — but it is not the only thing that matters. Your first job is primarily about learning, building skills, and establishing your professional reputation. The best investment you can make early in your career is in skills that will increase your market value significantly within 2–3 years.',
  'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1554224155-8d04cb21cd6c?w=800&q=80',
  'Career Advice',
  ARRAY['Salary', 'Freshers', 'Negotiation', 'Job Offer', 'Career Tips'],
  'Career Updates Team',
  'published',
  false,
  'How to Handle Salary Expectations as a Fresher | Career Updates',
  'Learn how to research salary benchmarks, answer the salary question confidently in interviews, and evaluate a job offer as a fresher in India.',
  now() - interval '11 days'
),

