-- ARTICLE 19: Offer Letter vs Appointment Letter
-- ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
(
  'Offer Letter vs Appointment Letter: What Is the Difference?',
  'offer-letter-vs-appointment-letter',
  'Many freshers use these terms interchangeably, but offer letters and appointment letters are different documents with different legal significance. Here is what each means and what to check in both.',
  E'# Offer Letter vs Appointment Letter: What Is the Difference?\n\nWhen you receive a job offer in India, you will typically encounter two documents: an offer letter and an appointment letter. Many freshers treat these as the same thing — but they are distinct documents issued at different stages of the hiring process, with different purposes and legal implications.\n\nUnderstanding what each document is — and what to look for in each — can prevent misunderstandings and protect you in your early career.\n\n## What Is an Offer Letter?\n\nAn offer letter is issued **after you have been selected**, typically at the end of the interview process. It is the company''s formal communication that they wish to hire you.\n\n### What an Offer Letter Typically Contains\n- The position you are being offered\n- The department or team\n- The proposed start date\n- The offered compensation (CTC breakdown)\n- Whether the offer is conditional (e.g., subject to background verification)\n- An expiry date by which you must accept the offer\n- Instructions on how to formally accept (signature, email response, or HR portal)\n\n### Important Things About the Offer Letter\n- It is a **conditional document** — it says "we want to hire you, subject to these conditions."\n- It does not confirm employment. It confirms *intent* to employ.\n- Background verification failures, document submission issues, or health check failures can result in the offer being withdrawn.\n- You should **read the offer letter carefully before signing** — particularly any conditions, the salary breakdown, and the joining deadline.\n\n## What Is an Appointment Letter?\n\nAn appointment letter is issued **after you have joined the company**, typically within the first few days of employment. It is a formal employment agreement that confirms you are now an employee.\n\n### What an Appointment Letter Typically Contains\n- Official designation and department\n- Date of joining\n- Detailed compensation and benefits breakdown\n- Working hours and leave entitlements\n- Probation period and its terms\n- Notice period requirements\n- Confidentiality and non-disclosure obligations (if applicable)\n- Code of conduct references\n- HR policies applicable to you\n\n### Important Things About the Appointment Letter\n- It is a **legal employment contract**. Keep a signed copy.\n- It supersedes the offer letter in the event of any discrepancy.\n- If anything in the appointment letter differs from the offer letter — especially the salary or role — raise this with HR immediately.\n- The notice period stated here is binding. Read it carefully.\n\n## Key Differences: Side by Side\n\n| Aspect | Offer Letter | Appointment Letter |\n|--------|-------------|-------------------|\n| When issued | After selection, before joining | After joining the company |\n| Purpose | Conditional intent to hire | Formal employment confirmation |\n| Legal status | Conditional, revocable | Binding employment contract |\n| Salary | CTC overview (sometimes) | Detailed breakdown |\n| Notice period | Often not included | Clearly stated |\n| Conditions | Often conditional | Confirmed/employment active |\n\n## What to Check in Your Offer Letter\n\n- [ ] Is the designation correct?\n- [ ] Is the CTC what was discussed in the interview?\n- [ ] Is the joining date reasonable?\n- [ ] What are the conditions for the offer (background check, documents)?\n- [ ] Is there a deadline to accept? Can it be extended?\n- [ ] What happens if you do not join after accepting?\n\n## What to Check in Your Appointment Letter\n\n- [ ] Does the salary breakup match what was in the offer letter?\n- [ ] What is the probation period and what happens during it?\n- [ ] What is the notice period?\n- [ ] Are the job title and department correct?\n- [ ] Are working hours and leave policy specified?\n- [ ] Are there any non-compete or confidentiality clauses that concern you?\n\n## Can an Offer Letter Be Revoked?\n\nYes. An offer letter can be revoked under certain conditions:\n- Failed background verification (criminal record, educational credential mismatch, employment history discrepancy)\n- Failure to submit required documents within the deadline\n- Failure to clear a medical examination (if required)\n- Business conditions change (company-wide hiring freeze)\n\nWhile offer rescissions by reputed companies are not common, they do happen. This is why you should:\n- Submit all required documents promptly and accurately\n- Not exaggerate credentials on your resume\n- Not resign from a current job until the offer is confirmed and the start date is approaching\n\n## What If There Is a Discrepancy Between the Two?\n\nIf your appointment letter shows a lower salary or a different role than what your offer letter stated, do not sign it without clarification. Request a meeting with HR and ask them to explain the discrepancy. Get any resolution in writing before signing.\n\n## Should You Accept Without Reading?\n\nNever. Many freshers, excited about their first job, sign offer and appointment letters without reading them. Read both documents completely. If there are terms you do not understand, ask HR to clarify. If there are terms that concern you, raise them professionally.\n\nYour appointment letter is an employment contract. Once signed, it is binding on both parties.',
  'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1450101499163-c8848c66ca85?w=800&q=80',
  'Career Advice',
  ARRAY['Offer Letter', 'Appointment Letter', 'Freshers', 'Jobs', 'Employment'],
  'Career Updates Team',
  'published',
  false,
  'Offer Letter vs Appointment Letter: What Is the Difference? | Career Updates',
  'Learn the key differences between offer letters and appointment letters in India — what to check in each and what to do if there is a discrepancy.',
  now() - interval '13 days'
),

