-- ARTICLE 20: How to Describe College Projects on a Resume
-- ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
(
  'How to Describe College Projects on a Resume',
  'how-to-describe-college-projects-on-resume',
  'College projects are one of the most important resume sections for freshers. Most students write them poorly. Here is how to describe your projects in a way that impresses recruiters.',
  E'# How to Describe College Projects on a Resume\n\nFor a fresher with no professional work experience, college projects are often the most important section of the resume. They are your proof of capability — evidence that you can apply your skills to a real challenge.\n\nUnfortunately, most freshers describe their projects in ways that fail to communicate their value. This guide shows you how to write project descriptions that make recruiters stop and pay attention.\n\n## Why Projects Matter So Much for Freshers\n\nWhen a recruiter reads a fresher resume, they are looking for signals that you can actually do the work. A list of technologies you "know" is not compelling. A project that used those technologies to solve a real problem is.\n\nA well-described project answers three questions:\n1. What problem did you solve?\n2. How did you solve it?\n3. What was the result?\n\n## The Common Mistake: What Most Freshers Write\n\n**Typical (weak) project description:**\n\n> **Student Result Management System**\n> A web application built using HTML, CSS, PHP, and MySQL.\n\nThis tells the recruiter almost nothing. What did it do? Why was it built? What did you specifically contribute? How many users does it have? What was interesting or challenging about it?\n\n## The Formula: What Strong Project Descriptions Look Like\n\nUse this structure for each project:\n\n```\n[Project Name] | [Tech Stack] | [Link to GitHub / Live Demo]\n- What it does (one line — the problem it solves)\n- What you specifically built (key features or components)\n- Technologies and how you used them\n- A result, metric, or challenge overcome\n```\n\n## Before and After Examples\n\n### Example 1: Software Project\n\n**Before:**\n> **Online Voting System**\n> Built using PHP and MySQL. Allows users to vote online.\n\n**After:**\n> **Online Voting System | PHP, MySQL, Bootstrap | github.com/name/voting-system**\n> - Built a secure web-based voting platform for college elections to replace a paper-based process.\n> - Implemented SHA-256 password hashing, session management, and one-vote-per-user enforcement.\n> - Designed an admin dashboard for real-time vote counting used by 200+ students during the college election.\n\n**Why it works:**\n- Explains the real-world problem (replacing paper voting)\n- Specifies security implementation\n- Provides a concrete scale (200+ users)\n\n### Example 2: Data Science Project\n\n**Before:**\n> **Sentiment Analysis Project**\n> Used Python and NLP to analyze Twitter data.\n\n**After:**\n> **Twitter Sentiment Analyser | Python, NLTK, Scikit-learn, Matplotlib | github.com/name/sentiment**\n> - Built a sentiment classification model on a dataset of 50,000 tweets to classify opinions as positive, negative, or neutral.\n> - Applied TF-IDF vectorization and trained a Naive Bayes classifier that achieved 82% accuracy on the test set.\n> - Visualized sentiment trends over time during a major news event using Matplotlib.\n\n### Example 3: Embedded Systems / ECE Project\n\n**Before:**\n> **Smart Street Light System**\n> Made using Arduino and sensors.\n\n**After:**\n> **Adaptive Street Lighting System | Arduino, PIR Sensor, LDR, Embedded C**\n> - Designed an automated street lighting system that reduces energy consumption by activating lights only when motion is detected during night hours.\n> - Programmed an Arduino Uno to interface with PIR and LDR sensors using I2C communication.\n> - Prototype demonstrated 35% reduction in active lighting time compared to always-on baseline during testing.\n\n## How to Add Numbers If You Think You Have None\n\nMany students believe their projects have no quantifiable results because they were academic. Look harder:\n\n- How many users or students used it?\n- What is the dataset size you worked with?\n- What accuracy or performance did your model achieve?\n- How much faster/slower was your solution versus the naive approach?\n- How many API endpoints did you build?\n- How many records does your database handle?\n- What was the load time improvement (if you optimized something)?\n\nEven small, honest numbers add credibility.\n\n## How Many Projects to Include\n\n- Include **2–4 projects** on your resume. More dilutes quality.\n- Choose projects that are **most relevant to the roles you are applying for**.\n- For technical roles, prioritize projects that use the specific tech stack the company uses.\n- Include your **final year / major project** if it is substantial.\n\n## Should You Include Group Projects?\n\nYes — but be honest about your contribution. Recruiters will ask "What specifically did you build in this project?" If you cannot answer clearly, they will know you were not the primary contributor.\n\nWhen describing a group project, clarify your role:\n> "Led the backend API development for this team project, building all REST endpoints and database schema."\n\n## What Not to Write\n\n- Do not list projects you cannot explain in an interview\n- Do not use passive voice: "A system was developed by me" → "Built a system"\n- Do not inflate the scale: do not claim 10,000 users if the project ran on localhost\n- Do not include incomplete projects unless you are transparent that they are in progress\n\n## Linking Your Projects\n\nAlways include a GitHub or live demo link. If the recruiter is reviewing your resume on a computer, they can click it. If there is no link, a recruiter has no way to verify your work.\n\nIf your project is not on GitHub, spend an hour uploading it. Write a clear README. This is one of the simplest things you can do to increase the credibility of your application.',
  'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1517694712202-14dd9538aa97?w=800&q=80',
  'Resume & CV',
  ARRAY['Resume', 'Projects', 'Freshers', 'GitHub', 'Career Tips'],
  'Career Updates Team',
  'published',
  false,
  'How to Describe College Projects on a Resume | Career Updates',
  'Most freshers write their resume project descriptions poorly. Learn the formula for compelling project entries with before/after examples.',
  now() - interval '14 days'
),

