-- ARTICLE 21: How to Prepare for a Walk-In Interview
-- ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
(
  'How to Prepare for a Walk-In Interview',
  'how-to-prepare-for-walk-in-interview',
  'Walk-in interviews are a common hiring format in India, especially at IT service companies. Here is what to expect and how to make the most of the opportunity.',
  E'# How to Prepare for a Walk-In Interview\n\nWalk-in interviews are one of the most common and accessible hiring formats in India, particularly for freshers and candidates with 0–2 years of experience. IT service companies — TCS, Infosys, Wipro, Capgemini, HCL, Cognizant, and many others — regularly conduct walk-in drives in major cities.\n\nUnlike scheduled interviews, walk-ins are open to anyone who arrives during the specified date and time window. This means more competition — but also more opportunity.\n\nHere is how to be prepared and stand out.\n\n## What Is a Walk-In Interview?\n\nA walk-in interview is a recruitment event where a company invites candidates to directly attend their office (or an event venue) during a specific window of time, without a prior appointment. Candidates arrive, submit their resume, and proceed through screening rounds — which may include an aptitude test, technical round, and HR round, all in the same day.\n\n## What to Expect at a Walk-In Drive\n\nThe typical format:\n\n1. **Registration** — You arrive, fill a form, and submit your resume and copies of your documents.\n2. **Group sorting** — Candidates may be grouped by qualification, experience level, or role applied for.\n3. **Aptitude / Written test** — Many companies start with a written test (quantitative aptitude, logical reasoning, verbal ability, and sometimes a technical test).\n4. **Technical interview** — One or two rounds with a technical panel assessing your knowledge and problem-solving.\n5. **HR interview** — Discussion about your background, salary expectations, joining availability, and location preferences.\n6. **Offer / Next steps** — Selected candidates may receive an on-the-spot offer or be told about next steps.\n\nNot all companies follow all these steps. Some walk-ins are purely for registration and shortlisting, with interviews scheduled later.\n\n## Before the Walk-In: Preparation\n\n### 1. Verify the details\nBefore going anywhere:\n- Confirm the date, time, and venue carefully\n- Check the eligibility criteria (percentage cutoff, graduation year, stream)\n- Confirm which documents to bring\n- Check whether registration is required online first (many companies now require pre-registration on their portal or on Naukri even for walk-ins)\n\n### 2. Carry the right documents\nBring multiple copies (at least 5–6) of:\n- Updated resume (printed, not handwritten)\n- Recent passport-size photographs\n- Aadhar card and PAN card (originals + copies)\n- All educational mark sheets (10th, 12th, degree semester-wise)\n- Degree certificate or provisional certificate\n- Internship or training certificates (if any)\n\nOrganise these in a folder or file cover. Disorganised document submission creates a negative impression.\n\n### 3. Prepare technically\nBrush up on:\n- Core subjects relevant to your degree\n- Basic aptitude (time, speed, percentages, probability, series)\n- Coding fundamentals if applying for a technical role\n- The company''s basic information (what they do, major clients, tech stack)\n\n### 4. Dress appropriately\nWalk-in interviews are formal. Dress in professional business-casual or formal attire:\n- Men: Formal shirt (tucked), formal trousers, leather shoes or clean formal footwear\n- Women: Formal kurta with trousers, saree, or formal western wear\n\nAvoid casual clothes, jeans, t-shirts, and flashy accessories.\n\n## At the Walk-In: What to Do\n\n### Arrive early\nArrive at least 30 minutes before your target time. Walk-ins can involve long queues. Arriving early gives you a queue advantage and reduces stress.\n\n### Be professional from the moment you arrive\nRecruiters observe candidates from the moment they enter — in the registration queue, in the waiting area, and everywhere. Behave professionally throughout.\n\n### Manage the wait\nWalk-in drives can involve significant waiting time — sometimes 2–4 hours or more. Bring a light snack or water if needed. Do not use your phone loudly or behave disruptively while waiting. Use the waiting time to review key concepts or review your resume.\n\n### In the aptitude round\nWork fast but accurately. Most aptitude tests are timed. If you are stuck on a question, move on and return if time allows.\n\n### In the technical round\nThink out loud. If you are not sure of an answer, explain your reasoning process. Honesty and structured thinking matter. Do not bluff.\n\n### In the HR round\nBe confident and honest. Have a clear answer for:\n- "Tell me about yourself"\n- "Why do you want to work here?"\n- "Are you comfortable relocating?"\n- "What is your expected salary?"\n- "When can you join?"\n\n## After the Walk-In\n\n- Note the interviewer''s name and contact if you can\n- If you are given a timeline for results, mark it in your calendar\n- Send a brief, polite follow-up email if you have the HR contact\n- Do not call repeatedly to follow up\n\n## Final Checklist for Walk-In Interviews\n\n- [ ] Date, venue, time, and eligibility confirmed\n- [ ] Online pre-registration completed (if required)\n- [ ] 5–6 copies of updated resume\n- [ ] Photos (passport size)\n- [ ] All original and photocopied documents in a folder\n- [ ] Dressed professionally\n- [ ] Arrived early\n- [ ] Technical concepts revised\n- [ ] Salary expectation answer prepared\n- [ ] Ready to discuss relocation and joining date\n\nWalk-in interviews are competitive, but they are also genuinely democratic — anyone who meets the eligibility criteria can attend. Preparation and professionalism are your differentiators.',
  'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1560472355-536de3962603?w=800&q=80',
  'Interview Prep',
  ARRAY['Walk-In Interview', 'Freshers', 'India', 'Interview Tips', 'Career'],
  'Career Updates Team',
  'published',
  false,
  'How to Prepare for a Walk-In Interview in India | Career Updates',
  'A complete guide to walk-in interviews in India — what to expect, what documents to bring, and how to stand out when competing with hundreds of candidates.',
  now() - interval '15 days'
),

