-- ARTICLE 23: Common Resume Mistakes Freshers Make in India
-- ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
(
  'Common Resume Mistakes Freshers Make in India (And How to Fix Them)',
  'common-resume-mistakes-freshers-india',
  'From fake skill bars to overly fancy templates, Indian freshers make predictable resume mistakes that cost them interviews. Here are the most common ones and exactly how to fix each.',
  E'# Common Resume Mistakes Freshers Make in India (And How to Fix Them)\n\nAn Indian recruiter reviewing fresher applications in a day might see hundreds of resumes. Many of them make the same predictable mistakes. These mistakes do not just reduce your chances — some are immediate disqualifiers.\n\nThis guide covers the most common resume mistakes made by Indian freshers, with specific fixes for each.\n\n## Mistake 1: Using a Canva or Fancy Graphic Template\n\n**What it looks like:** Two-column layouts with coloured sidebars, skill progress bars (Python: 80%), circular profile photos, icons, and elaborate formatting.\n\n**Why it is a problem:** Applicant Tracking Systems (ATS) — the software many companies use to parse and filter resumes — cannot read multi-column layouts, tables, and graphics correctly. Your resume gets garbled in the database and may never reach a human reviewer. Even if it reaches a human, recruiters at many companies find heavily formatted resumes difficult to scan quickly.\n\n**The fix:** Use a clean, single-column text-based template. Create it in Microsoft Word, Google Docs, or LaTeX. Use consistent formatting, clean section headers, and save as PDF.\n\n## Mistake 2: Skill Progress Bars and Percentages\n\n**What it looks like:** "Python ▓▓▓▓▓░░░░░ 70%", "Java ▓▓▓▓▓▓▓░░░ 80%"\n\n**Why it is a problem:** Skill percentages are meaningless and unverifiable. What does 70% in Python mean? How does your 70% compare to someone else''s 70%? Recruiters know these numbers are made up. They add no information.\n\n**The fix:** List your skills plainly: "Python, Django, REST APIs, MySQL, Git." If you want to indicate proficiency, you can use categories like "Proficient in: ... | Familiar with: ..."\n\n## Mistake 3: An "Objective Statement" That Says Nothing\n\n**What it looks like:** "To obtain a challenging position in a reputed organization where I can utilize my skills for mutual growth and development."\n\n**Why it is a problem:** This is on thousands of resumes. It says nothing about you specifically. It is the resume equivalent of filler text.\n\n**The fix:** Replace it with a 2–3 line professional summary that is specific to your background and target role. Name your degree, mention a project or skill, and state what you are looking for.\n\n## Mistake 4: Including Every Technology You Have Ever Heard Of\n\n**What it looks like:** Skills section with 30+ items: "HTML, CSS, JavaScript, React, Angular, Vue, Node.js, Python, Java, C, C++, PHP, Laravel, Django, Flask, Machine Learning, Deep Learning, TensorFlow, PyTorch, MATLAB, R, Arduino, VLSI, AutoCAD, SolidWorks..."\n\n**Why it is a problem:** Listing technologies you cannot actually discuss in an interview is a trap. Interviewers will pick something from your skills list and ask about it. If you cannot answer, you have lost credibility on everything on the list.\n\n**The fix:** List only skills you can genuinely answer questions about and demonstrate through projects. Be honest.\n\n## Mistake 5: No GitHub or Project Links\n\n**What it looks like:** "Projects" section with project names, descriptions, and tech stack — but no links.\n\n**Why it is a problem:** Recruiters at tech companies want to see the code. "I built a React app" is a claim. A GitHub link with a working repo is evidence.\n\n**The fix:** Upload every project to GitHub with a clear README. Add the link directly on the resume next to each project.\n\n## Mistake 6: Including a Photo\n\n**What it looks like:** A passport-size or profile photo in the corner of the resume.\n\n**Why it is a problem:** In most modern professional contexts — especially IT companies in India''s metro cities and any international application — a photo on a resume is unnecessary and can introduce bias in shortlisting. It also takes up space.\n\n**The exception:** Some fields (hospitality, media, certain government applications) do require a photo. Know your industry.\n\n**The fix:** Remove the photo unless the job application explicitly requires one.\n\n## Mistake 7: No Quantification in Project Descriptions\n\n**What it looks like:** "Built a web application for managing student records using PHP and MySQL."\n\n**Why it is a problem:** This describes what you built, not what it achieved or what challenges you solved.\n\n**The fix:** Add numbers wherever possible. "Built a web application for managing student records used by 300+ students and 20 faculty members, reducing administrative processing time from 2 days to 2 hours."\n\n## Mistake 8: Listing Responsibilities Instead of Achievements\n\n**What it looks like:** "Responsible for frontend development" / "Was part of the team that built the authentication module"\n\n**Why it is a problem:** Responsibilities tell a recruiter what your role was, not what you contributed. Passive language ("responsible for," "was part of") further dilutes impact.\n\n**The fix:** Use active verbs and focus on what you built or achieved: "Built the entire frontend UI for the authentication module using React, reducing login errors by 25% through improved form validation."\n\n## Mistake 9: Inconsistent Formatting\n\n**What it looks like:** Different fonts in different sections, mixed date formats (01/2024 vs January 2024 vs Jan 24), inconsistent bullet styles, varying indentation.\n\n**Why it is a problem:** Inconsistent formatting makes a resume look careless and unprofessional. If you cannot format a document carefully, it raises doubts about your attention to detail in actual work.\n\n**The fix:** Use a single font throughout, consistent date formats, uniform bullet styles, and equal margin spacing. Review the final PDF carefully before sending.\n\n## Mistake 10: Two Pages When One Is Enough\n\n**What it looks like:** A two-page resume with significant whitespace, long project descriptions, or irrelevant information padded to fill the space.\n\n**Why it is a problem:** Freshers with 0–2 years of experience do not have enough content to justify two pages. A recruiter who sees a two-page fresher resume assumes poor judgement about relevance and brevity.\n\n**The fix:** Cut ruthlessly. Keep it to one page. If something does not directly support your application for this specific role, remove it.\n\n## Quick Fix Checklist\n\n- [ ] Single-column, clean template (no Canva graphics)\n- [ ] No skill progress bars or percentages\n- [ ] Specific professional summary (not a generic objective)\n- [ ] Only skills you can genuinely discuss\n- [ ] GitHub links on all technical projects\n- [ ] No photo (unless required)\n- [ ] Numbers in project descriptions\n- [ ] Active verbs, achievement-focused language\n- [ ] Consistent formatting throughout\n- [ ] One page total',
  'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1586281380349-632531db7ed4?w=800&q=80',
  'Resume & CV',
  ARRAY['Resume', 'Freshers', 'Mistakes', 'Career Tips', 'India'],
  'Career Updates Team',
  'published',
  false,
  'Common Resume Mistakes Freshers Make in India | Career Updates',
  'Avoid these 10 resume mistakes that cost Indian freshers interviews — from fancy Canva templates to meaningless skill bars and vague project descriptions.',
  now() - interval '17 days'
),

