-- ARTICLE 26: One-Page vs Two-Page Resume for Freshers
-- ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
(
  'One-Page Resume vs Two-Page Resume: Which Should Freshers Use?',
  'one-page-vs-two-page-resume-freshers',
  'There is a persistent debate about resume length. For freshers in India, the answer is clear — and here is why.',
  E'# One-Page Resume vs Two-Page Resume: Which Should Freshers Use?\n\nThe debate about resume length has been ongoing for decades. You will find career advisors who swear by one page and others who say two pages is fine for anyone with a few years of experience.\n\nFor freshers — specifically Indian students and recent graduates with 0–1 years of professional experience — the answer is unambiguous: **use one page.**\n\nThis article explains why, what to do if your content spills onto two pages, and the only exceptions where a second page might be justified.\n\n## Why One Page for Freshers\n\n### 1. You Do Not Have Enough Content for Two Pages\n\nA two-page resume is justified when a candidate has extensive, relevant professional experience, achievements, publications, or leadership roles that cannot reasonably be condensed.\n\nAs a fresher, you likely have:\n- 3–4 years of education\n- A few projects\n- Possibly one internship\n- Some certifications and extracurriculars\n\nThis does not fill two pages with genuinely useful content. Two-page fresher resumes are almost always padded — with large fonts, excessive whitespace, inflated descriptions, and irrelevant content.\n\n### 2. Recruiters Scan, They Do Not Read\n\nResume research consistently finds that recruiters spend very little time on the initial pass of any single resume. A two-page resume does not give you more time — it gives you less. Anything on page two is much less likely to be seen than anything on page one.\n\n### 3. It Signals Poor Judgement\n\nThe ability to communicate clearly and concisely is a professional skill. A fresher who cannot fit their profile into one page signals poor judgement about relevance and priority — which is itself a signal to a recruiter.\n\n## What to Cut If You Are Running Over One Page\n\nIf your resume spills onto a second page, here is what to cut:\n\n**Cut first:**\n- An "objective statement" (replace with a short summary or cut entirely)\n- High school marks (10th and 12th marks are rarely needed unless specified in the job requirements)\n- Hobbies and personal interests (unless directly relevant to the role)\n- "References available on request" — remove it; this is assumed\n- Long project descriptions — tighten to 3 bullet points maximum per project\n\n**Cut next:**\n- Certifications that are not relevant to the target role\n- Coursework that is already implied by your degree\n- Soft skills that are too generic ("good communicator," "team player")\n- Redundant skills (listing Python, Django, AND Flask when the job only needs one)\n\n**Formatting adjustments that can help:**\n- Reduce font size from 12pt to 10.5pt or 11pt\n- Reduce margins slightly (0.5 inch instead of 1 inch)\n- Use single line spacing within sections, with slightly more space between sections\n- Use a tighter bullet style\n\n## What About Two Pages for Engineers With Strong Projects?\n\nOccasionally, a fresher with genuinely exceptional depth — multiple substantial projects, research publications, or a significant internship at a top company — might legitimately struggle to fit on one page.\n\nIn this case, two pages is acceptable only if:\n- Every single line of content adds value\n- There is no padding or filler whatsoever\n- Page two contains real, substantive content — not just overflow\n\nThe test: if you removed page two, would a recruiter miss anything essential? If no, keep it to one page.\n\n## The Exception: Academic Resumes / CVs\n\nIf you are applying for research positions, graduate school programs, or academic roles, you may be asked for a CV (Curriculum Vitae) rather than a resume. CVs are longer by convention and include more comprehensive education and publication details. The one-page rule applies to professional resumes, not academic CVs.\n\n## Summary\n\n| Experience Level | Recommended Length |\n|---|---|\n| Fresher (0–1 year) | 1 page |\n| Early career (1–5 years) | 1–2 pages |\n| Mid-career (5–10 years) | 2 pages |\n| Senior (10+ years) | 2–3 pages |\n| Academic / Research | 2–5+ pages (CV format) |\n\n## Final Thought\n\nA tightly written, well-organized one-page resume that is easy to scan demonstrates exactly the kind of clear thinking that recruiters want to see in a new hire. It is not a limitation — it is an opportunity to show that you know what matters.',
  'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1586282391129-76a6df230234?w=800&q=80',
  'Resume & CV',
  ARRAY['Resume', 'Freshers', 'Career Tips', 'Resume Length'],
  'Career Updates Team',
  'published',
  false,
  'One-Page vs Two-Page Resume for Freshers | Career Updates',
  'Should freshers use a one-page or two-page resume? The answer is clear — and here is how to fit everything valuable onto one page.',
  now() - interval '20 days'
),

