-- ARTICLE 27: What to Do Before, During, and After an Online Interview
-- ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
(
  'What to Do Before, During, and After an Online Interview',
  'before-during-after-online-interview',
  'Online interviews have become standard in India. Here is a practical checklist for everything you need to do before, during, and after a virtual interview to give yourself the best chance.',
  E'# What to Do Before, During, and After an Online Interview\n\nOnline interviews — via Google Meet, Zoom, Microsoft Teams, or company-specific platforms — are now standard practice for fresher hiring across India. Many companies conduct their entire hiring process virtually, including aptitude tests, technical rounds, and HR interviews.\n\nThe fundamentals of interviewing remain the same, but online interviews have their own specific preparation requirements. Getting the technical setup wrong can hurt your chances regardless of how well prepared you are academically.\n\n## BEFORE the Interview\n\n### 1. Confirm the Details\n\nAt least 24 hours before the interview:\n- Verify the date, time, and time zone (especially for companies with offices in different zones)\n- Confirm the platform (Zoom, Meet, Teams, custom link)\n- Confirm what you will need: is it a video call, or might they send you a coding test on a specific platform?\n- Check whether you need to download any software or register on any platform before the call\n\n### 2. Test Your Technology\n\n**Internet connection:**\n- Use a wired connection (Ethernet) if possible — it is far more stable than Wi-Fi\n- If Wi-Fi is your only option, sit close to the router\n- Run a speed test at fast.com to confirm your connection is stable\n- Avoid scheduling interviews at times when multiple people in your house are using bandwidth heavily\n\n**Audio:**\n- Test your microphone — can you be heard clearly?\n- Use earphones or headphones with a built-in microphone; they produce cleaner audio than a laptop mic\n- Test in a quiet environment for echo and background noise\n\n**Camera:**\n- Ensure the camera is at eye level — not angled up from below\n- Check the lighting: sit facing a window or a light source, not with it behind you (backlighting makes you appear as a silhouette)\n- Check what is visible in the background — keep it clean, neutral, and professional\n\n**Platform:**\n- Log into the interview platform at least 10–15 minutes early\n- Join a test call if the platform offers one to confirm your audio and video settings work\n\n### 3. Prepare Your Environment\n\n- Choose a quiet location where you will not be interrupted during the interview\n- Inform people in your household not to disturb you during the interview window\n- Put your phone on silent (not vibrate)\n- Close unnecessary browser tabs and applications before the interview starts\n- Disable notifications (email, Slack, WhatsApp, system updates)\n\n### 4. Prepare Your Materials\n\nHave ready and visible (near your screen, not on your screen):\n- A printed copy of your resume\n- Notepad and pen for notes\n- A list of your prepared questions to ask the interviewer\n- Any links you might need to reference (GitHub, portfolio, project demos)\n\n### 5. Dress Professionally\n\nDress the same way you would for an in-person interview. This matters for two reasons:\n1. It creates a professional impression\n2. It puts you in the right mental state for the interview\n\n## DURING the Interview\n\n### 6. Join Early\n\nJoin the call 5 minutes before the scheduled time. Being late to an online interview — even by 5 minutes — creates an immediately negative first impression.\n\n### 7. Camera and Eye Contact\n\nLook at the camera, not at the interviewer''s face on your screen. Looking at the face on screen means your eyes appear to be looking downward or to the side on their end. Looking at the camera creates the impression of genuine eye contact.\n\nThis is a small adjustment that makes a significant difference in how engaged and confident you appear.\n\n### 8. Speak Clearly and at the Right Pace\n\nNetwork latency can cause slight audio delays in online calls. Speak slightly more slowly than you would in person. Pause briefly after finishing a thought to avoid talking over the interviewer.\n\n### 9. Handle Technical Issues Gracefully\n\nIf your connection drops or there is audio trouble:\n- Stay calm — technical issues happen and interviewers understand this\n- Reconnect to the call as quickly as possible\n- Apologise briefly: "Apologies for the interruption — my connection dropped for a moment."\n- If the issue persists, have the interviewer''s email ready so you can offer to reschedule or move to a phone call\n\n### 10. Avoid These Common Online Interview Mistakes\n\n- Typing or clicking loudly while the interviewer is speaking\n- Having distracting objects or movement in your background\n- Reading answers from off-screen notes too obviously\n- Eating or drinking during the interview\n- Allowing pets or other household members to enter the frame\n- Looking at yourself on the screen instead of at the camera\n\n## AFTER the Interview\n\n### 11. Send a Thank-You Email\n\nWithin 24 hours of the interview, send a brief, professional thank-you email to the interviewer (or to HR, if that is your point of contact):\n\n> "Thank you for taking the time to speak with me today. I really enjoyed learning more about [specific thing discussed in the interview]. I remain very interested in the role and I look forward to next steps."\n\nKeep it short — 3–5 sentences. This is a professional courtesy that very few candidates bother with, so it creates a positive impression.\n\n### 12. Note What Went Well and What Did Not\n\nImmediately after the interview, while it is fresh:\n- What questions were you asked?\n- Which answers did you feel confident about?\n- Which questions caught you unprepared?\n- What would you answer differently?\n\nThis reflection improves your performance in subsequent interviews, even if this one leads to an offer.\n\n### 13. Follow Up Appropriately\n\nIf you were given a specific timeline for a decision ("We will get back to you within a week"), wait until that deadline has passed before following up. Then send one polite enquiry email.\n\nDo not send multiple follow-up messages or call repeatedly. Patience and professionalism matter.\n\n## Online Interview Checklist\n\n**Before:**\n- [ ] Details confirmed (date, time, platform, link)\n- [ ] Internet connection tested and stable\n- [ ] Audio tested (microphone and earphones)\n- [ ] Camera at eye level, lighting is good\n- [ ] Background clean and professional\n- [ ] Environment quiet and private\n- [ ] Phone on silent, notifications disabled\n- [ ] Browser tabs minimised, unnecessary apps closed\n- [ ] Resume, notepad, and pen ready\n- [ ] Platform logged in 10 minutes early\n\n**During:**\n- [ ] Joined 5 minutes early\n- [ ] Looking at camera, not screen\n- [ ] Speaking clearly and at a controlled pace\n- [ ] Professional dress\n- [ ] Engaged and attentive\n\n**After:**\n- [ ] Thank-you email sent within 24 hours\n- [ ] Interview notes written down\n- [ ] Follow-up timeline tracked',
  'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1588196749597-9ff075ee6b5b?w=800&q=80',
  'Interview Prep',
  ARRAY['Online Interview', 'Virtual Interview', 'Freshers', 'Interview Tips', 'Career'],
  'Career Updates Team',
  'published',
  false,
  'What to Do Before, During, and After an Online Interview | Career Updates',
  'A complete checklist for virtual interviews in India — technical setup, during-interview tips, and the post-interview follow-up that most candidates skip.',
  now() - interval '21 days'
),

