-- ARTICLE 28: How to Evaluate a Job Offer Before Accepting
-- ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
(
  'How to Evaluate a Job Offer Before Accepting It',
  'how-to-evaluate-job-offer-before-accepting',
  'Receiving a job offer is exciting — but signing without thinking it through can lead to regret. Here is a structured framework for evaluating any job offer before you say yes.',
  E'# How to Evaluate a Job Offer Before Accepting It\n\nReceiving a job offer — especially your first one — is exciting. The natural impulse is to accept immediately.\n\nBut a few days of thoughtful evaluation can prevent months or years of regret. Before you sign, take time to assess the offer against what actually matters for your career and lifestyle.\n\nThis guide gives you a structured framework to evaluate any job offer clearly.\n\n## Before You Evaluate: Give Yourself Space\n\nMost companies give you 1–5 days to decide on an offer. Use that time. Thank them for the offer, confirm you will respond by the deadline, and then evaluate calmly.\n\nA company that pressures you to accept immediately ("This offer expires tonight") is showing you something about how they operate.\n\n## Dimension 1: Compensation\n\n### Understand the full CTC breakdown\nAsk for a complete salary breakup. The headline CTC number includes components with different practical values:\n\n- **Basic salary** — The taxable base\n- **HRA (House Rent Allowance)** — Tax-advantaged if you are renting in a city\n- **Special allowance** — Usually fully taxable\n- **Variable / performance bonus** — Only paid if targets are met; not guaranteed\n- **Employer PF contribution** — Goes into your Provident Fund account, not your bank monthly\n- **Other benefits** — Medical insurance, food allowance, transport, etc.\n\nA ₹5 LPA CTC with a large variable component and significant PF deduction may result in a lower monthly in-hand salary than expected. Calculate the actual take-home before comparing offers.\n\n### Research market rates\nCompare the offer against current market rates for:\n- Your role\n- Your location\n- Your experience / skills level\n\nUse AmbitionBox, Glassdoor, and Naukri Salary Insights. If the offer is significantly below market, it is worth understanding why — or whether there is room to negotiate.\n\n### Consider growth, not just the starting number\nA ₹3.5 LPA starting salary at a company that promotes people quickly and gives strong annual increments may be worth more than a ₹4.5 LPA starting salary at a company with flat salaries for years.\n\n## Dimension 2: Role and Responsibilities\n\nBefore accepting, confirm:\n- What will your actual day-to-day work look like?\n- What team or project will you be assigned to?\n- Will you be working on the things mentioned in the job description, or doing something entirely different?\n\nIf you were hired for software development but you suspect you will be doing data entry or testing, ask directly. It is better to know before you join.\n\n## Dimension 3: Learning and Growth\n\nYour first job''s biggest long-term value is what you learn. Consider:\n\n- **What skills will you build?** Working at a company with strong engineering practices will make you a significantly better engineer than one where you write similar boilerplate code for years.\n- **How quickly do people get promoted?** Ask during the HR round.\n- **Do they invest in employee development?** Training budgets, conference attendance, learning subscriptions.\n- **What is the career trajectory of people who join at your level?** Where are they in two to three years?\n\n## Dimension 4: Company Stability and Culture\n\n- **Is the company financially stable?** Check if the company has had recent layoffs, funding issues, or public financial difficulties.\n- **What do current employees say?** Check Glassdoor and AmbitionBox reviews. Look for patterns (not individual complaints) in themes like management, work-life balance, and compensation.\n- **What is the management culture like?** Did you get a sense of the team in the interview? Ask to speak with someone at a similar level if you have questions.\n\n## Dimension 5: Location and Work Arrangement\n\n- **Where is the office?** Factor in commute time and cost.\n- **Is remote or hybrid work available?** What is the company''s policy?\n- **Will you be asked to relocate?** Is the location somewhere you can live and work happily?\n\n## Dimension 6: Benefits and Leave\n\n- **Health insurance:** Does the offer include medical insurance, and what does it cover?\n- **Leave policy:** How many earned leaves, casual leaves, and sick leaves per year?\n- **Public holidays:** Does the company follow national holidays?\n- **Notice period:** What is the notice period if you decide to leave? Long notice periods (3 months or more) can be a constraint.\n\n## Dimension 7: The Offer Relative to Your Alternatives\n\nEvaluate the offer not just in absolute terms but relative to your other options:\n- Do you have other offers to compare against?\n- Are you in the middle of interviews with other companies that might result in a better offer?\n- Is this your only offer after a long search?\n\nIf you have competing offers, it is acceptable and professional to inform a company: "I have received another offer and need to make a decision. Could we confirm the timeline?" This is not manipulative — it is a normal professional conversation.\n\n## Making the Decision\n\nUse a simple scoring framework:\n\n| Factor | Weight | Company A | Company B |\n|--------|--------|-----------|----------|\n| Compensation | 20% | /10 | /10 |\n| Role relevance | 20% | /10 | /10 |\n| Learning opportunity | 25% | /10 | /10 |\n| Company stability | 15% | /10 | /10 |\n| Location / work arrangement | 10% | /10 | /10 |\n| Benefits | 10% | /10 | /10 |\n\nAdjust the weights based on what matters most to you at this stage of your career.\n\n## A Final Note on Declining Offers\n\nIf you decide not to accept an offer, decline professionally and promptly. Do not simply go silent. Thank them genuinely, explain briefly that you have decided to pursue another opportunity, and wish them well.\n\nThe Indian professional world is smaller than it appears. A polite decline is remembered; ghosting a company is also remembered — for the wrong reasons.',
  'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1554224155-8d04cb21cd6c?w=800&q=80',
  'Career Advice',
  ARRAY['Job Offer', 'Salary', 'Freshers', 'Career Decisions', 'Employment'],
  'Career Updates Team',
  'published',
  false,
  'How to Evaluate a Job Offer Before Accepting It | Career Updates',
  'A structured framework to evaluate any job offer — compensation, role, learning, company stability, and location — before you say yes.',
  now() - interval '22 days'
),

-- ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
-- ARTICLE 29: How to Read a Job Description Before Applying
-- ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
(
  'How to Read a Job Description Before Applying',
  'how-to-read-job-description-before-applying',
  'Most freshers either ignore job descriptions or skim them briefly. Reading one properly takes five minutes and dramatically improves your application quality. Here is exactly how to do it.',
  E'# How to Read a Job Description Before Applying\n\nThe job description is the most important document in any job search — and most freshers read it carelessly or not at all.\n\nReading a job description properly takes five minutes. Those five minutes determine whether you tailor your resume and cover letter effectively, whether you apply for a role you are actually qualified for, and whether you go into the interview with genuine preparation.\n\nHere is how to read a job description the way a careful professional does.\n\n## Why Most Freshers Read Job Descriptions Poorly\n\nThe common fresher approach:\n1. See the job title\n2. Read the company name\n3. Check the location\n4. Click "Apply Now"\n\nThis approach treats every job as the same. It results in generic applications, poor match rates, and wasted interview preparation.\n\n## A Framework for Reading a Job Description\n\nBreak the job description into five parts and extract information from each.\n\n### Part 1: The Role\n\n**What to look for:**\n- The actual job title (is it what you thought it was?)\n- The team or department\n- The level (intern, fresher, junior, associate, engineer)\n- Who it reports to\n\n**Questions to ask yourself:**\n- Is this actually the role I want, or is it a different job with a similar title?\n- Is this an individual contributor role or a team lead role?\n- Is the level appropriate for my experience?\n\n**Example — notice the difference:**\n- "Junior Software Engineer — Products Team" → You will build features on a product\n- "Software Engineer — Internal Tools" → You will build internal tooling for the company\n- "Associate Engineer — IT Support" → You may be doing user-support, not product development\n\nThese are very different roles. The title alone does not distinguish them.\n\n### Part 2: Responsibilities\n\nThis tells you what you will actually do every day. Read it carefully.\n\n**What to look for:**\n- Are these responsibilities you are excited about?\n- Are there responsibilities that surprise you — that you did not expect for this role?\n- Are there responsibilities you cannot perform or are not interested in?\n\n**Example responsibilities to read carefully:**\n- "Occasional client interaction" → This may involve presentations and calls with clients\n- "Cross-functional collaboration" → This is a team-intensive role\n- "Maintain and update legacy codebase" → You will work with old code, not greenfield development\n- "On-call support rotation" → You may need to respond to incidents outside business hours\n\nNone of these are bad — but you should know what you are applying for.\n\n### Part 3: Required Qualifications vs Nice-to-Have\n\nMost job descriptions list two types of qualifications:\n- **Required (must-have):** "B.Tech/B.E. in Computer Science or related field," "Proficiency in Python," "Experience with REST APIs"\n- **Preferred (good-to-have):** "Familiarity with Docker," "Experience with microservices architecture"\n\n**The practical rule:**\nIf you meet 60–70% of the required qualifications, apply. You do not need to meet 100% of the nice-to-haves.\n\nFreshers often disqualify themselves from roles they could get by assuming they need to meet every listed requirement. Many requirements on job descriptions are aspirational. Apply if you are a reasonable fit.\n\n**What to extract:**\n- A list of required skills → check them against your resume\n- A list of preferred skills → these are areas to mention if you have any exposure\n- These keywords → they should appear in your tailored resume and cover letter\n\n### Part 4: Keywords for Resume and Interview Tailoring\n\nJob descriptions are written using specific terminology that the team uses. Use those same terms.\n\nIf the JD says "REST APIs," use "REST APIs" on your resume — not "web services" or "HTTP endpoints."\n\nIf the JD says "Agile development environment," mention your experience with Agile or Scrum.\n\nThis alignment matters because:\n1. ATS systems search for keyword matches\n2. Interviewers pick questions based on what you claim\n3. It signals that you read and understood the role\n\n### Part 5: Company Information and Context\n\nMany job descriptions include information about the company''s product, culture, or values. Read it.\n\n- What does the product do? Who uses it?\n- What does the team''s mission statement say?\n- What values or culture signals are embedded in the description? ("We value ownership," "Move fast," "Data-driven")\n\nThis information is directly useful for:\n- "Why do you want to work here?" (interview question)\n- Tailoring your cover letter opening\n- Preparing relevant questions to ask the interviewer\n\n## A Job Description Reading Checklist\n\n- [ ] What is the actual role (not just the title)?\n- [ ] What will I do day-to-day?\n- [ ] Are there any responsibilities that concern or surprise me?\n- [ ] What are the required qualifications? Do I meet 60%+?\n- [ ] What are the preferred qualifications?\n- [ ] What keywords appear that I should use in my resume?\n- [ ] What does this role tell me about the company''s product or team?\n- [ ] What questions should I ask in the interview based on this description?\n\n## When to Not Apply\n\nDo not apply if:\n- The role is significantly outside your current capability (less than 40% of requirements met)\n- The responsibilities describe a kind of work you have no interest in\n- The location or work arrangement does not work for you\n- There are compensation or structure issues in the listing that are dealbreakers\n\nApplying to every listing regardless of fit wastes time — yours and the recruiter''s. Quality over volume is almost always more effective.\n\n## Final Thought\n\nA job description is a written specification of what the employer needs. Your application — resume, cover letter, interview answers — should be a direct response to that specification. You cannot write that response if you have not read the document carefully first.',
  'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1454165804606-c3d57bc86b40?w=800&q=80',
  'Job Search',
  ARRAY['Job Description', 'Job Search', 'Freshers', 'Application Tips', 'Career'],
  'Career Updates Team',
  'published',
  false,
  'How to Read a Job Description Before Applying | Career Updates',
  'A five-part framework for reading job descriptions properly — extract the role, responsibilities, required skills, keywords, and company context to apply more effectively.',
  now() - interval '23 days'
),

